#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157051 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"}] :question/id #uuid "6a492cbf-d378-5193-baf2-c88a2bea63fb" :question/math-academy-id "e-21774" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 6y = (3x+1)\\sin(x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, what is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution $y_p(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 6y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157052 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/id #uuid "9a26313d-4b93-5d82-98c1-dbecb0bead82" :question/math-academy-id "e-21775" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 5y = \\cos 2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form  ![](images/ae/ae29bceba4d6a1f250f244b26583abeb4b8c2fbe33d380dfa4332b051bb21078.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x) =$ ![](images/c6/c6725d6cd732c1ca7f21ab9a8dac245bc396c04a623cfb8e2eda31751ddaa89e.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$  ![](images/1d/1d1c8a545fb741413f7cdd2f6f3fa6190fbdda74290e206cc4a7f8061f97ff53.png)." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 5y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{5x})}^{′}-5(u{e}^{5x}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+5u{e}^{5x}-5u{e}^{5x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x}+\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}}-\\cancel{5u{e}^{5x}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{5x} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{5x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{5x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{5x} \\\\  & ={e}^{5x}\\int {e}^{-5x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157053 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/id #uuid "bafa3bf1-a867-5259-a6da-226195b45541" :question/math-academy-id "e-21777" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} - 3y = 12\\sinh 3x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\qquad$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form  ![](images/ae/ae29bceba4d6a1f250f244b26583abeb4b8c2fbe33d380dfa4332b051bb21078.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x) =$  ![](images/c6/c6725d6cd732c1ca7f21ab9a8dac245bc396c04a623cfb8e2eda31751ddaa89e.png).\n\n$\\qquad$ Therefore, the particular solution is $y_p(x) =$  ![](images/1d/1d1c8a545fb741413f7cdd2f6f3fa6190fbdda74290e206cc4a7f8061f97ff53.png)." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution $y_c,$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\ny_c' - 3y_c = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c  = Ae^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable $y_p,$ we set $A = u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = u(x)e^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of $y_p$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate $u'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{3x})}^{′}-3(u{e}^{3x}) & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x}-3u{e}^{3x} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}}-\\cancel{3u{e}^{3x}} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{3x} & =12\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\sinh 3x = \\dfrac{e^{3x} - e^{-3x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =12{e}^{-3x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡3x \\\\  & =12{e}^{-3x}(\\frac{{e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}}{2}) \\\\  & =6{e}^{-3x}({e}^{3x}-{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =6-6{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =6-6{e}^{-6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 6-6{e}^{-6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =6x+{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $y_p = u(x)e^{3x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{3x} \\\\  & =6x{e}^{3x}+{e}^{-3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157756 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157456 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ac34a18-053d-5eab-8c2c-bd0f15658186" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using Variation of Parameters"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f5d2375-e461-5779-bd70-2d097de2bd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309745} {:answer/id #uuid "97ad96b3-c48c-559e-90f8-02d18d9ab028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309966} {:answer/id #uuid "b8efe656-9f3f-5736-ad5d-c094fa0bb0c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310246} {:answer/id #uuid "bc4429cf-1848-53bf-a57c-a12b8724b777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310273} {:answer/id #uuid "e75e533a-d00e-543a-91f0-6beda7657e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc4429cf-1848-53bf-a57c-a12b8724b777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310273} :answer-field/id #uuid "2475f97e-6754-596e-90d9-a74d9cec482b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310884} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "017b6b6e-3683-5fbe-8d33-d98b097694c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-10x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308755} {:answer/id #uuid "44673883-8db5-5131-a5de-37696508b30d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309269} {:answer/id #uuid "93573567-69bf-5595-a25f-d51a975bc247" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309926} {:answer/id #uuid "a9af85f4-19bc-5a56-8fbc-27067af74132" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-8x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310121} {:answer/id #uuid "c753e576-59af-5b1b-9469-fe5aec59c6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310349}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c753e576-59af-5b1b-9469-fe5aec59c6f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310349} :answer-field/id #uuid "278141e9-4e04-5c9c-ab26-31d26f986a2b" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310893} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23c689cb-4205-5595-87e7-3b4ff544bb75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309018} {:answer/id #uuid "2bcb3173-bcdd-56e5-8331-f8cc5e3912a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309076} {:answer/id #uuid "380e805a-be4a-54b4-a1ca-492bfda4bfe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309174} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee5420b-306f-564c-8248-d04f61935aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309458} {:answer/id #uuid "96a0e52a-84d9-53e1-a4c8-60125f8ec45a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309957}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bcb3173-bcdd-56e5-8331-f8cc5e3912a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309076} :answer-field/id #uuid "f46fb3e1-c358-54d4-ba7b-332d199867ef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311506}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "62e0a162-13e2-59de-92b2-13df782b20dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-337174" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+4y={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below. \n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-4x})}^{′}+4(u{e}^{-4x}) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x}+4u{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{-4x}} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-4x} & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{6x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{6x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{6x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{6x}\\cdot {e}^{-4x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157758 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e52d783-e1b0-550c-9ad9-28901f11b559" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308845} {:answer/id #uuid "1f4d10f9-7065-514e-b58d-de71f1962a8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308991} {:answer/id #uuid "ddef27cb-c10e-59f9-894a-53d879f3e8fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310528} {:answer/id #uuid "fd6c11e4-f725-5074-9d66-3e1b7bcc8952" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310778}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddef27cb-c10e-59f9-894a-53d879f3e8fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310528} :answer-field/id #uuid "29c175d6-9963-5d59-966c-5c33b922f542" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310902} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c9425e5-7ab8-51aa-9668-a7fc95ba7630" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308971} {:answer/id #uuid "27b055ad-d73f-5947-8464-35e3f3b20b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309049} {:answer/id #uuid "343e9519-d793-50b1-80a1-3b6b3c8bf5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309145} {:answer/id #uuid "5a2f1dd7-0d9e-5fe9-a61e-bf5771b359fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309425} {:answer/id #uuid "796ed93d-4b82-5ed3-82ff-94c8b9a6d861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309696} {:answer/id #uuid "e589a667-b374-5228-bee2-c2350b473468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "796ed93d-4b82-5ed3-82ff-94c8b9a6d861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309696} :answer-field/id #uuid "e89d636e-b274-5467-befa-750fc7f0a317" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1964814c-72a7-5d83-834d-b63441ad6011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308950} {:answer/id #uuid "1f7d4ed5-c0cc-52c8-8817-60e735537443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308992} {:answer/id #uuid "28586268-06c7-558d-998c-b7f35a63c10f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309055} {:answer/id #uuid "b284cf2e-4efe-5048-bc5f-455b42e8f168" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310191} {:answer/id #uuid "c9202501-a8cc-53a0-b861-5074a47aa78e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f7d4ed5-c0cc-52c8-8817-60e735537443" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308992} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9d8d958-ca6a-534b-a36e-14f918f29429" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "171f0292-ff02-54bb-a27d-4ba2f7ec9f11" :question/math-academy-id "q-337177" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-4y={e}^{4x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below. \n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{4x})}^{′}-4(u{e}^{4x}) & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x}-4u{e}^{4x} & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}} & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x} & ={e}^{4x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{4x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157760 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82bcf901-4bc6-5ba1-9f79-9f2789a29f3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(x) = u(x)e^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155479} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ee0cb0-e30c-50f1-a981-7511437600f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(x) = u(x)e^{-6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155480} {:answer/id #uuid "cadcffe3-f2ec-5379-bc3f-c226a6a783d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(x) = u(x)e^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155481} {:answer/id #uuid "09621aeb-e967-5fcf-bb63-f8cbe37d860e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(x) = u(x)e^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155482} {:answer/id #uuid "5815d38f-1957-5664-8353-147badad6e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p}(x) = u(x)e^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155483}] :answer-field/id #uuid "8e2d776b-916a-506e-bc6f-9af1b018f8f4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186155478}] :question/id #uuid "bc1320a7-5bac-56be-bf06-3e780f4ac1ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-337251" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} + 6y = x^{2} - 3x + 1$.\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$?"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157455 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f1059ab6-d512-537f-be1e-28aa492a6ef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Suitable Variation of Parameters Ansatz"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1292c1fe-b11a-58a7-8f4d-286dc46ca316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308882} {:answer/id #uuid "62e564a8-1a14-51b7-b9ce-cf9a08d16f02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309499} {:answer/id #uuid "6798ea5a-ea80-5e39-909d-37a6af80560d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309541} {:answer/id #uuid "d3c7d778-f422-51be-b3bd-27b1e893a13b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310457} {:answer/id #uuid "f37b6567-980c-5cb8-84da-be82c7d6ee79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f37b6567-980c-5cb8-84da-be82c7d6ee79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310682} :answer-field/id #uuid "75439944-6cdb-57ee-9ada-15c6be91338b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "565a9d25-9f82-568f-a358-819477f18aab" :question/math-academy-id "q-337356" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=({x}^{2}+2)\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2x).\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397061 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e403ce47-06c5-5e9b-ab8e-462b81d03f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397063} {:answer/id #uuid "11a32a7f-7b9c-504f-b1d3-2e76cef86def" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397064} {:answer/id #uuid "96b52573-5826-593c-8432-290b29b2304f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397065} {:answer/id #uuid "0ff820c1-181b-5e4b-acbc-aa742b9a3b31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397066} {:answer/id #uuid "1ea5bfac-c767-5b9d-ab68-68b54a6358b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397067} {:answer/id #uuid "4222ee29-c478-5b04-aa00-976fed70c609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4222ee29-c478-5b04-aa00-976fed70c609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397068} :answer-field/id #uuid "abaac5ec-4860-5fab-abd5-9e1c5fafe889" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397062} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79ff23f2-915d-5d8b-9114-3779b44c89bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397070} {:answer/id #uuid "a584cd0d-27c6-531c-970d-493932778886" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397071} {:answer/id #uuid "38447498-29d3-5175-a4df-a73a990ee625" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{5x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397072} {:answer/id #uuid "0124f381-e063-50ed-b5a1-3c3f9da3ad9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397073} {:answer/id #uuid "603df059-0fa2-51f8-bca9-c190d6e5b9ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0124f381-e063-50ed-b5a1-3c3f9da3ad9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397073} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2f973d5-c3c3-591c-86ce-78dde5c8e25a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397069} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7665d218-e973-5ccf-a112-1b3cee61a4b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397076} {:answer/id #uuid "2a5fbc5d-982a-5e37-a3dc-84a876e64fc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397077} {:answer/id #uuid "b7470fb6-388e-532f-839c-4263ebaddc37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397078} {:answer/id #uuid "9448a899-f5a3-5f84-b46b-10313439f92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7665d218-e973-5ccf-a112-1b3cee61a4b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x{e}^{-7x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397076} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d0b526f-0a36-505c-bd42-a28d844cfb3c" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34254624-9c90-5339-836d-8f0fcd78b2d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-337469" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+7y={e}^{-7x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-7x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-7x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-7x})}^{′}+7(u{e}^{-7x}) & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x}-7u{e}^{-7x}+7u{e}^{-7x} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x}-\\cancel{7u{e}^{-7x}}+\\cancel{7u{e}^{-7x}} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-7x} & ={e}^{-7x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-7x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-7x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18284b2b-4baa-542f-b3e9-f9b101400186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{6x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308940} {:answer/id #uuid "1ff5d425-2d91-50b8-9bcc-9692165610b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308993} {:answer/id #uuid "53b8cc38-caf9-50a1-a118-c87e677f7809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309383} {:answer/id #uuid "68df02b9-4559-5126-84a0-ba28cb02fc19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309555} {:answer/id #uuid "ac205e92-9455-5d91-bfba-6e02ad69a22d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310140}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53b8cc38-caf9-50a1-a118-c87e677f7809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u(x){e}^{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309383} :answer-field/id #uuid "464e3dff-e0cc-5e85-ad7a-58ca73b13764" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310982} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "554c57fc-e876-56b7-94d6-d7df7bf38bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309392} {:answer/id #uuid "597f39be-9a1f-5d38-9861-17c41bcc4e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}+2{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309422} {:answer/id #uuid "5e975f0c-6327-5a3e-9a4a-3ba400131efe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}+x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309456} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe953db-5211-5d7f-8fac-fc01e86f8803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "554c57fc-e876-56b7-94d6-d7df7bf38bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309392} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d7d836f-6d18-5bf8-a30a-8cd709e799d5" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311082} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "149d4e7b-e129-51ce-9d5c-05e86d24d773" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186308902} {:answer/id #uuid "8e85b76c-5f96-59bb-a728-d1a1b2826e2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+2x{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309882} {:answer/id #uuid "a8782715-ec59-53ed-8453-10ff273baec7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+2{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310107} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3a9ec6-48f6-5fda-9b04-48714a713242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310624}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec3a9ec6-48f6-5fda-9b04-48714a713242" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4x+{e}^{-4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186310624} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6871c91-599d-5c8a-9961-f82fb77091f1" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186311465}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43a060a8-89ee-5d43-a547-481f4dd7a9ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-337518" :question/problem "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y=8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{2x})}^{′}-2(u{e}^{2x}) & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x}-2u{e}^{2x} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}}-\\cancel{2u{e}^{2x}} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{2x} & =8\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\ {u}^{′} & =8{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=\\frac{{e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}}{2}\n$$\n\nour equation for $u$ becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =8{e}^{-2x}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x \\\\  & =8{e}^{-2x}(\\frac{{e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}}{2}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{-2x}({e}^{2x}-{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =4-4{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =4-4{e}^{-4x} \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 4-4{e}^{-4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =4x+{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{2x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{2x} \\\\  & =4x{e}^{2x}+{e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157457 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5fb547a-2518-5db9-b1b1-aab8d80328af" :knowledge-point/title "Solving First-Order Linear ODEs Using VOP When the Forcing Term is Part of the Complementary Solution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24bd1c52-2c1a-5a9b-8db0-abe912c62f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer